    <h1>SEER2 Explained: What Efficiency Ratings Actually Save You</h1>
    <p class="intro-lead">
      Contractors often pitch high-efficiency upgrades by promising your electric bill will be cut in half. Before paying $3,000 extra for a higher SEER2 badge—or factoring utility savings into your replacement math—here are the verified federal rules and real kilowatt-hour arithmetic.
    </p>

    <h2>Verified DOE Regional Minimums (10 CFR 430.32)</h2>
    <p>
      Effective <strong>January 1, 2023</strong>, the U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) updated residential central air conditioner and heat pump efficiency standards under <strong>10 CFR 430.32</strong> and switched the test procedure from SEER/HSPF to <strong>SEER2/HSPF2</strong>, which uses higher external static pressure to better reflect real home ductwork.
    </p>

